<div class="prose reveal">
<p class="eyebrow on-dark">Verify it yourself</p>
<h2 class="h2">What Permit #64666 actually verifies.</h2>
<div class="rule"></div>
<p>Miami-Dade County chauffeur registration isn't a badge we bought — it's a public record. Getting one requires a background check, a clean driving record on file with the county, and annual renewal to stay active. It's issued and tracked by Miami-Dade County's Department of Transportation and Public Works, independently of anything we say about ourselves.</p>
<p>Most operators in this market write "licensed and insured" without a number attached. We put ours in the footer of every page for one reason: it's checkable, and we'd rather you check it than take our word for it.</p>
</div>
<div class="figure reveal">
<div class="kicker">Permit<br>#64666<br><em>public record</em></div>
<p>Miami-Dade County Department of Transportation and Public Works, Passenger Transportation Regulatory Division.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="section-soft">
<div class="wrap">
<p class="eyebrow">The comparison worth making</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:22px">A confirmed fare vs. an app's estimate.</h2>
<div class="wrap split" style="padding:0">
<div class="prose reveal">
<h3 style="font-size:19px;margin-bottom:10px">A rideshare estimate</h3>
<ul class="points">
<li><i class="mark"></i><span>The number on screen when you request is not the number when the car arrives — demand pricing can move it in either direction up to the moment of pickup.</span></li>
<li><i class="mark"></i><span>The driver and vehicle are assigned at request time, from whoever is nearest and available.</span></li>
<li><i class="mark"></i><span>Cancellations and driver reassignment are common in the minutes before pickup, especially at airports during peak arrival banks.</span></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="prose reveal">
<h3 style="font-size:19px;margin-bottom:10px">A confirmed fare</h3>
<ul class="points">
<li><i class="mark"></i><span>The number you're quoted is the number you pay, agreed before the vehicle is dispatched — not adjusted in transit.</span></li>
<li><i class="mark"></i><span>The same vehicle and standard, held for your reservation specifically, not pulled from a shared pool.</span></li>
<li><i class="mark"></i><span>One phone number if a flight changes or a meeting runs long — not a support ticket.</span></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<p style="color:var(--muted);margin-top:22px;max-width:640px">Neither model is wrong — they solve different problems. A rideshare app is built for availability at a moment's notice. A confirmed fare is built for a specific date you can't afford to leave to chance.</p>
</div>
</section>

